      ],
      "trims": [],
      "engines": [],
      "category": "engine",
      "title": "Oil-Bathed Timing Belt Degradation Causing Oil Starvation (1.0 Ti-VCT 3-Cylinder)",
      "description": "The Brazilian-built Ka III's 1.0 Ti-VCT three-cylinder engine uses a wet timing belt that runs immersed in engine oil, with a nominal replacement interval of 260,000 km. Brazilian mechanics and the automotive press document a chronic failure pattern: when oil changes are stretched or oil outside Ford's 5W-20 specification is used, the belt's rubber degrades and sheds debris into the sump. The debris clogs the oil pump pickup strainer, causing low oil pressure, hydraulic lifter noise, accelerated wear of bearings and cylinder walls, overheating, and in advanced cases complete engine failure requiring a rebuild. Repair shops report rebuild costs that can approach the market value of the car. The same engine and failure mode also affects the Brazilian EcoSport.",
      "solution": "Strictly follow oil changes every 10,000 km/6 months with Ford-spec SAE 5W-20 (every 5,000 km/3 months under severe use such as city-only driving). Many Brazilian mechanics recommend replacing the wet belt preventively around 100,000-150,000 km rather than the 260,000 km factory interval. If lifter noise or low oil pressure appears, immediately inspect and clean the oil pump pickup strainer and check for belt debris in the sump; engines run long with a clogged pickup typically need bearing replacement or a full rebuild.",
      "severity": "high",
      "confidence": "high",
      "symptoms": [
        "Ticking or knocking noise from the engine, especially at cold start",
        "Low oil pressure warning light",
        "Excessive vibration at idle",
        "Rubber or metallic debris found in the oil at oil changes",
        "Engine overheating or loss of power in advanced cases"
      ],
